// Expected: e0
//
// Two unrelated sub-protocols. Alice and Bob exchange a MAC that Bob checks;
// the tag is unguarded, so the attacker can tamper with it and halt Bob on the
// failed check. Carol and Dave separately run a guarded Diffie-Hellman, and
// their derived keys agree by commutativity, so the equivalence query holds.
//
// Halting Bob must not decide Carol and Dave's query. When a checked primitive
// fails, `reexec::drop_after_index` truncates that principal's slots past the
// failure, and `resolve_constant` answers for a slot that is no longer there
// with the bare constant. Comparing that bare constant against the other side's
// real value reports a divergence nobody computed: a false attack, and the
// worst class of regression this engine has. This model pins the guard in
// `query_equivalence` that keeps a halted principal from answering for values
// it never reached.
//
// Before that guard, this model reported e1 with a trace whose only step was
// the attacker replacing `tag` -- a value that appears nowhere in `ck` or `dk`.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private mk
	generates m
	tag = MAC(mk, m)
]

Alice -> Bob: m, tag

principal Bob[
	knows private mk
	_ = ASSERT(tag, MAC(mk, m))?
]

principal Carol[
	generates c
	gc = PUBKEY(c)
]

principal Dave[
	generates d
	gd = PUBKEY(d)
]

Carol -> Dave: [gc]
Dave -> Carol: [gd]

principal Carol[
	ck = DH_KEX(gd, c)
]

principal Dave[
	dk = DH_KEX(gc, d)
]

queries[
	equivalence? ck, dk
]
